{
  "abstract": "We study the 38-vertex, 193-edge nine-critical biplanar graph of lunar_structure, whose private host and carrier have 17 and 19 vertices. Choose any equally sized subsets of the two interiors and identify them by an arbitrary bijection, leaving the original terminals unchanged and adding no edges. We prove that every biplanar quotient obtained in this way still has chromatic number nine. The identification need not preserve induced adjacency, and no embedding hypothesis is imposed. Exact edge accounting excludes twelve or more pairs. Positive boundary-coloring menus handle up to six pairs. For seven through eleven pairs, density bounds the number of host edges newly imposed on the carrier, and explicit host symmetries reduce the extension problem to eight exceptions. A bank of 22 carrier colorings repairs six of them; complete injection enumeration handles the remaining two. An intermediate theorem shows that adding at most eight edges to this fixed private carrier leaves an eight-colorable graph or creates a literal nonbiplanar obstruction: a nine-clique or a Hajós sum of two nine-cliques. All finite premises have compact direct checkers using literal positive colorings, complete branching, and independently reconstructed graphs. The portable replay needs only Python and a standard C++17 compiler, and completes in about seven seconds. The theorem closes one complete fixed-source construction family; further contacts, noninjective mergers, other sources, and the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem remain outside its scope.",
